<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 128–<a href="/years/63-bce">63 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Metellus Pius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius was a Roman general and politician, a supporter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> and consul with him in 80 BCE. As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-ulterior">Hispania Ulterior</a> from 79 BCE he fought the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius">Quintus Sertorius</a> for most of a decade, later alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, until the war ended in 72 BCE. He was pontifex maximus from 81 BCE until his death, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-pius.jpg" alt="Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Published by Guillaume Rouille (1518?-1589) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quintus_Metellus_Pius.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Metellus Pius belonged to the plebeian gens Caecilia and was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-numidicus">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Numidicus</a>, consul in 109 BCE. He accompanied his father to Numidia during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurthine-war">Jugurthine War</a>. When Numidicus was banished through the manoeuvres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a> and Lucius Appuleius Saturninus, the son campaigned for his recall, petitioning in 99 BCE, and in 98 BCE the tribune Quintus Calidius carried a law that allowed Numidicus to return. His persistence earned him the nickname Pius. In the 90s BCE he was elected to the College of Pontiffs, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a> he served as a legate, winning battles against the Marsi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elected praetor in 88 BCE, Metellus Pius enrolled the Italian allies as Roman citizens and then took the southern front, capturing Venusia and defeating the rebel leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-poppaedius-silo">Quintus Poppaedius Silo</a>. When Cinna and Marius took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 87 BCE he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and acted as governor there until the Marians sent out Gaius Fabius Hadrianus in 84 BCE, who drove him out. By 83 BCE he had joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Lucius Cornelius Sulla</a>, who made him his principal subordinate, and the Senate declared him a public enemy. In 82 BCE he defeated Marian forces in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, with the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> as his cavalry commander. Sulla made him pontifex maximus in 81 BCE and consul beside himself in 80 BCE.</p><p>In 79 BCE the Senate sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-ulterior">Hispania Ulterior</a> to fight the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius">Quintus Sertorius</a>. Sertorius's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics defeated Metellus repeatedly, and by 77 BCE the Senate sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> with a large army to help. Metellus defeated Sertorius's legate Lucius Hirtuleius at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italica">Italica</a> in 75 BCE, and was acclaimed imperator after a battle near Segontia in which he was wounded by a spear. He offered 100 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> and 20,000 acres to any Roman who would betray Sertorius, who was murdered in 72 BCE. His governorship ended in 71 BCE, and he held a triumph with Pompey on 30 December 71 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the 60s BCE Metellus Pius opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>'s extraordinary commands, and he was one of the former consuls called as witnesses against Pompey's associate Gaius Cornelius when he was charged with maiestas. He was a friend and patron of the poet Aulus Licinius Archias. He died around 63 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> succeeded him that year as pontifex maximus. He had no natural children, and by his will he adopted a son of Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica, who became Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius Scipio Nasica, consul in 52 BCE and father-in-law of Pompey.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The final phase came in the Sertorian War of 80–72 BCE, when the Roman rebel Quintus Sertorius won the support of Lusitanians and Celtiberians and resisted the armies of <b>Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius</b> and Pompey for several years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Worn down by <b>Metellus Pius</b> and Pompey, his alliance frayed.</blockquote><a href="/state/sertorian-state">Sertorian State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-86" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 3947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 3947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-86">What was here in 86 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
